Notebook[{
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Quit", "[", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.986431562027958*^9, 3.986431563954569*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[1]:=",ExpressionUUID->"093eb5c2-d7aa-734e-938a-7e274eca08e1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["README:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nThis Notebook contains the calculations performed in Appendix C of \
arXiv:2605.16168.\n\nIn that article, we find solutions to consistent \
truncations of supergravity where some real scalars are analytically extended \
to imaginary values, ensuring the metric remains real-valued. Among the \
solutions there are Lorentzian traversable wormholes connecting two \
asymptotically Anti-de Sitter spacetimes and flows that have a real metric \
also when uplifted to ten or eleven dimensions. We argue that the holographic \
duals are pseudo-Hermitian and PT-symmetric theories. Wormhole solutions also \
admit an interpretation as the low-energy theory of two stacks of branes and \
antibranes after tachyon condensation. The wormhole is then dual to an \
entangled state of two copies of the theory that lives on a stack of branes. \
We present some evidence by computing the mutual information between the \
theories at each boundary and by identifying the Goldstone bosons associated \
to the breaking of the two copies of Poincar\[EAcute] symmetry to their \
diagonal subgroup.\n\nEven though the solutions we construct satisfy a set of \
BPS equations and can be seen as analytic extensions of supersymmetric \
solutions, their stability is not guaranteed. In this Notebook we study the \
perturbative stability of 3 examples of ghost scalars coupled to gravity, \
listed in the section 2.2 of arXiv:2605.16168. \n\nIn the Examples 1 and 2 \
that we examine, we find no evidence of instability. In Example 5 we find an \
unstable mode.\n\nThe methodology is briefly explained at the start of each \
example and in much more detail in Appendix B and C of arXiv:2605.16168.\n\n\
Authors of the article: Jose M. Begines, Ant\[OAcute]n F. Faedo, Carlos \
Hoyos, Daniele Musso\nDevelopers of the Notebook: Carlos Hoyos, Daniele Musso"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.998535668083417*^9, {3.998535731175661*^9, 3.99853577566446*^9}, {
   3.998536545278207*^9, 3.998536553861557*^9}, 3.998536835580852*^9, {
   3.998537496805833*^9, 3.998537497197109*^9}, {4.0039730781407475`*^9, 
   4.003973107784649*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d507a637-ac91-470c-b604-\
7436c90bf79b"],

