Cell["Stability Analysis", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9864315513728848`*^9, 3.98643155768684*^9}, {
  3.9864316502411423`*^9, 
  3.986431655591076*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3e271d52-3df3-0641-946c-\
5e3dbf8685e8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "d+1=5 Wormholes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[ScriptCapitalN]", "\[LongEqual]", "4"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"9cd61dcf-83d0-074f-a012-5073bbccf7bb"],
 " SYM Coulomb branch (Example 1)"
}], "Subchapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9833416864867673`*^9, 3.983341690357538*^9}, {
  3.9857665885783463`*^9, 3.985766592501463*^9}, {3.985786819918578*^9, 
  3.9857868245576534`*^9}, {3.9864316656248093`*^9, 3.986431669617153*^9}, {
  3.986431820042404*^9, 3.9864318209528008`*^9}, {3.9864329704712143`*^9, 
  3.9864329781513805`*^9}, {3.986458253017193*^9, 3.9864582611208496`*^9}, {
  3.986572006599554*^9, 3.986572006799473*^9}, {3.9985366109361134`*^9, 
  3.998536614393983*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7e07631d-d8de-5148-87dc-\
190eed5e2357"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Methodology:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nWe employ a spectral method based on Chebyshev polynomials.\n\nWe first \
compactify the radial domain introducing a coordinate \[Upsilon] \[Element] \
[-1,1]. The resulting ODE has a singular point at \[Upsilon] = 0, which \
requires us to split the problem into two subdomains, \[Upsilon] \[Element] \
[-1,0] and \[Upsilon] \[Element] [0,1]. Since the two subproblems are \
identical, we restrict our analysis to the negative-\[Upsilon] domain. The \
solution in the positive-\[Upsilon]  domain is determined by parity.\n\nWe \
then analyze the asymptotic behavior of the solutions at the two endpoints, \
\[Upsilon] = -1 and \[Upsilon] = 0. This allows us to explicitly factor out \
the corresponding asymptotic behavior at each endpoint. We subsequently \
introduce a new radial coordinate s \[Element] [-1,1], which provides the \
standard domain for the Chebyshev expansion. \n\nThe radial domain is \
discretized on a Gauss-Lobatto grid. This yields a convenient and efficient \
construction of discretized radial first- and second-order derivatives. Since \
the regular asymptotics have been factored out, no additional boundary \
conditions need to be imposed. The spectrum is then obtained by solving the \
resulting discretized generalized eigenvalue problem."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.998535802085884*^9, 3.998535842105816*^9}, {
  3.9985359459652357`*^9, 3.998536098730426*^9}, {4.0039731655011673`*^9, 
  4.003973203228233*^9}, {4.0039751999464283`*^9, 
  4.0039752313271303`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"54c6664f-7d99-494d-80d9-\
da906af83dfb"],

