Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["d+1=4 Wormholes ABJM Coulomb branch (Example 2)", "Subchapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9833416864867673`*^9, 3.983341690357538*^9}, {
  3.9857665885783463`*^9, 3.985766592501463*^9}, {3.985786819918578*^9, 
  3.9857868245576534`*^9}, {3.9857919876762733`*^9, 3.9857919878555813`*^9}, {
  3.9864316719367733`*^9, 3.986431674711916*^9}, {3.9864317325622425`*^9, 
  3.9864318036071663`*^9}, {3.986432960817375*^9, 3.986432974124449*^9}, {
  3.986458277863806*^9, 3.9864582807094193`*^9}, {3.9865720088153534`*^9, 
  3.9865720089147205`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7c7bf0ea-e3b8-d94a-a5d6-\
8f61fbe17f4f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Methodology:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nThis example can be treated analytically.\n\nWe first compactify the \
radial domain introducing a coordinate \[Upsilon] \[Element] [-1,1]. The \
resulting ODE has a singular point at \[Upsilon] = 0, which requires us to \
split the problem into two subdomains, \[Upsilon] \[Element] [-1,0] and \
\[Upsilon] \[Element] [0,1]. Since the two subproblems are identical, we \
restrict our analysis to the negative-\[Upsilon] domain. The solution in the \
positive-\[Upsilon]  domain is determined by parity.\n\nThe resulting ODE can \
be solved analytically (not all versions of Mathematica are able to find the \
analytical solution: we employed Mathematica 11, while more recent versions, \
such as Mathematica 13 and 14, did not find an analytical solution). \n\nThe \
spectrum is obtained by imposing  a fixed parity of the solutions  and a \
vanishing source at the boundary (in the alternative quantization)."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.998535802085884*^9, 3.998535842105816*^9}, {
   3.9985359459652357`*^9, 3.998536098730426*^9}, {3.998536138374627*^9, 
   3.998536240027849*^9}, 3.998536812995895*^9, {4.0039745153479486`*^9, 
   4.003974555810608*^9}, {4.0039751639536777`*^9, 
   4.0039751658610315`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c6d7bb3f-83c7-4235-8df0-\
6d6154130f60"],

